<h1>📊 Full Method Comparison: IDW vs. Ordinary Kriging</h1>

<table>
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="width:18%;">Aspect</th>
            <th style="width:27%;">IDW</th>
            <th style="width:30%;">Ordinary Kriging</th>
            <th style="width:25%;">✅ Why IDW is Selected</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td><strong>Core Idea</strong></td>
            <td>Closer points receive larger weights</td>
            <td>Models spatial structure, then optimal prediction</td>
            <td>Intuitive and easy to explain in 4‑slide PPT</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><strong>Semivariogram Required</strong></td>
            <td><span class="cross">✗</span> No</td>
            <td><span class="check">✓</span> Yes, needs fitting</td>
            <td>Reduces extra assumptions</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><strong>Parameters</strong></td>
            <td>Power parameter θ</td>
            <td>Nugget, sill, range, variogram model</td>
            <td>Only one main parameter, easy to tune via LOOCV</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><strong>Interpretability</strong></td>
            <td>High: larger θ = stronger nearby influence</td>
            <td>Medium‑high: professional but heavier</td>
            <td>Reviewers quickly grasp "nearby neighborhoods are more similar"</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><strong>Implementation Complexity</strong></td>
            <td><span class="badge">Low</span></td>
            <td><span class="badge" style="background:#f59e0b;">Medium‑High</span></td>
            <td>More stable, less affected by unstable variogram fitting</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><strong>Data Requirement</strong></td>
            <td>Low; works with limited samples</td>
            <td>High; needs clear spatial structure</td>
            <td>Only 140 centroid points → IDW is better suited</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><strong>Prediction Output</strong></td>
            <td>Smoothed risk surface</td>
            <td>Smoothed risk surface + prediction variance</td>
            <td>Strict LOOCV shows IDW and spherical kriging are effectively tied</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><strong>Uncertainty Representation</strong></td>
            <td>Not directly; LOOCV error approximates</td>
            <td><span class="check">✓</span> Direct Kriging variance output</td>
            <td>Kriging serves as supplementary benchmark</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><strong>Computational Efficiency</strong></td>
            <td><span class="badge">Fast</span></td>
            <td>Slow</td>
            <td>Suitable for rapid tuning and cross‑validation</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><strong>Outlier Sensitivity</strong></td>
            <td>Sensitive, especially with large θ</td>
            <td>Also sensitive, but nugget can mitigate</td>
            <td>LOOCV‑based θ selection controls over‑reliance on nearest neighbors</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><strong>Suitable Scenarios</strong></td>
            <td>Quick interpolation, risk mapping, interpretable prediction</td>
            <td>Rigorous geostatistical modeling, uncertainty estimation</td>
            <td>Goal: interpretable, presentable, verifiable risk map</td>
        </tr>
        <tr class="highlight">
            <td><strong>Presentation Impact</strong></td>
            <td>Simple and direct</td>
            <td>More advanced and statistical</td>
            <td><strong>IDW as primary, Kriging as advanced benchmark — balanced</strong></td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<div class="conclusion">
    <strong>✅ Conclusion:</strong> After all models were scored on the same target (<code>log(theft_count + 1)</code>)
    using strict LOOCV, with kriging variograms refit inside each held-out fold,
    <strong>Kriging (Spherical, RMSE 0.6875) and IDW (θ=3, RMSE 0.6898) are effectively tied</strong>.<br>
    With fewer assumptions, transparent parameter tuning, and stable behavior for 140 centroid observations,
    <strong>IDW is selected as the primary model</strong>; Kriging remains the closest geostatistical benchmark and provides complementary uncertainty estimates.
</div>

